  "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 13 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 7 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 7 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
